  method 1(To check whether a distribution is Gaussian or not)

let X is any continuous random varaible .
X---->it is having a 500 observations/values 
X----->x1,x2,x3,x4------------------x500 

X follows any distribution  
task:check X follows GD or not? 

sort the X in ascending order 
   x1,x2,x3-----------------------x500 --->500 data points
  Sorting is important because quantiles depend on ordered data.

   take a percentile of X 
   
   x5',x10',x3'-------------------x500 --->100  datapoints.
   
Create Theoretical Normal Values (Y)
Now Python imagines what the data would look like if it were perfectly 
Gaussian.
These are not real data values — they come from the normal 
distribution formula.

So it creates theoretical normal quantiles:
let Y is continuous RV 
  Y~GD(mean,SD)---->1000 observations 
  Y=y1,y2,y3----------------------y1000--->1000 datapoints 
  sort Y in ascending order 
  y1,y2,y3-----------------------------------y1000 

  y10',y20'---------------------------------y1000'-->100 datapoints.
 
 X-axis → theoretical normal values (Y)
 Y-axis → actual data values (X) 
here  why not actual data in x axis and theory in y ?
cause  Theoretical distribution (normal distribution) → reference line
Sample data → what we are testing
as we compare sample with thery proven value 


X--->with 100 datapoints(percentile )
Y--->with 100 datapoints(percentile ) 


we will draw a graph between X percentile and Y percentile 
if we get a straight line then we can say that distribution is  
a Gaussian .
-if all the points of X and Y are lies in a SL(straight line) then we can 
say that X follows the GD .
